f 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1045 "Linear Dependence and Independence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ea872253-9665-5737-9ff4-e1c4c5546bda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120300 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477587 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186120300 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477588 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186120300 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477589 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186120300 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34153" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120300 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120300 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- **II.** The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- **III.** The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186120300 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd1b519a-6daa-58c6-a554-f53dcb225d1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120300 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186120318 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477590 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186120318 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477591 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186120318 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477592 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186120318 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120318 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120318 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$  has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n- **II.** The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- **III.** The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186120318 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "202bc30a-b4f4-567b-8021-1d6c51e8b85e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120318 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statement III is false, while statements I and II are true." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186120320 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120320 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120320 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120320 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120320 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9695b9e5-18af-5741-bfa4-d11e2760dae4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120320 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]=-4[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=-4{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}-4{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=4$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120877 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120877 #edb/ref 1061 41352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120877 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8b84b116-3328-5055-9d5b-02360cff09e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120877 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120878 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120878 #edb/ref 1061 41353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120878 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e814b4bf-688c-57df-8849-f4f2455723dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120878 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120879 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120879 #edb/ref 1061 41354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120879 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "efae78f1-ab53-50ee-aa00-035536697ae6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120879 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120880 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120880 #edb/ref 1061 41357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120880 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46556bbf-0804-5a49-af3c-3a10e60ee378" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120880 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120881 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120881 #edb/ref 1061 41358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120881 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "50295cf6-d08a-5be4-b71d-2116227de4f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120881 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120882 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120882 #edb/ref 1061 41359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120882 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8247f0d4-142e-58a3-b2ed-d612accf2a6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120882 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120883 #edb/ref 1061 41362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120883 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4922cdd4-be71-550d-b216-e5a30b232248" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120883 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121122 #edb/ref 1064 "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = 0,$ then we say that the two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly dependent**.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider two sets of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/46/46c22d599752ded730a67bfd57b4dfe238e512f6dff2d561afaf2f145c8118c2.svg)\n\n- For the first set ${\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is true only if $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ is *linearly independent*.\n\n- On the other hand, for the second set ${\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has solutions other than $x_1=x_2=0.$ For example, $x_1=2$ and $x_2=1$ gives\n![](images/14/145f500e491087587478f03bd52ae180a7b4237acf17db0317ca07d28f389c56.svg)\nTherefore, the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ is *not* linearly independent. Rather, it is *linearly dependent*.\n\n**Note:** You may notice that the vectors in the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ were parallel (i.e. multiples of one another), while the vectors in set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ were not. This trend holds in general, for all sets of two vectors:\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel, while\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly independent* if and only if the two vectors are not parallel." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121122 #edb/ref 1065 6811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121122 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "938aba59-31f8-5ad9-8d3d-ce9287feb3de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121122 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121123 #edb/ref 1064 "Any set of two vectors that contains the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ must be linearly dependent. To see this, consider the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.$ We can realize that this set is linearly dependent using either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} + 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v} + x_2 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a solution $x_1=0$ and $x_2=1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set is linearly dependent.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, recall that a set of two vectors is linearly dependent if and only if the two vectors are parallel. That is, the two vectors are multiples of each other.\n\nThe zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to every other vector because it is a multiple of any other vector. For any vector $\\mathbf{v},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{0} = 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\mathbf{0} \\parallel \\mathbf{v}.\n$$\n\nSo, because $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to $\\mathbf{v},$ the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121123 #edb/ref 1065 6812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121123 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ef2c96dd-d1cd-593e-a998-ba1be6c42686" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121123 #edb/ref 1067 "Sets of Two Vectors Containing the Zero Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121124 #edb/ref 1064 "So far, we've discussed linear dependence and linear independence for sets of two vectors. However, these concepts can be extended to sets of any number of vectors.\n\nA set of $n$ vectors $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\right\\}$ is called **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + x_n \\mathbf{v}_n = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1=x_2=\\ldots=x_n=0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = \\ldots = x_n = 0,$ then we say that the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\right\\}$ is **linearly dependent**.\n\nSimilar to sets containing two vectors, a general set of $n$ vectors is dependent if *any* two vectors in the set are parallel.\n\n**But be careful!** When working with sets containing more than two vectors, having a linearly dependent set is possible even if *none* of the vectors are parallel! \n\nFor example, consider the following set of vectors: \n\n![](images/bd/bd4df860628c44295dfd621e6b0e64839c2657c2baa094def656ea8b90ece9c8.svg)\n\nNone of the vectors in this set are parallel. However, we have\n\n![](images/50/5048e293fd6bb60a0ca95451aebd3bb77d0f748bb54e9f5dd044f60fb34c28ce.svg)\n\nSo, the equation\n\n![](images/8a/8ab4013ae0d9241268d040220efcd6184e23548e0685802cd6825b59a7bc71b1.svg)\n\nhas a solution $x_1 = 1,$ $x_2 = 1,$ and $x_3 = -1,$ which is different from $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that the vectors are linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121124 #edb/ref 1065 6813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121124 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0d550a61-4219-5ffc-83a2-155ddd8fa9f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121124 #edb/ref 1067 "Linear Dependence and Independence for a General Set of Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121125 #edb/ref 1064 "As with sets containing only two vectors, any set of vectors that contains the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ must be linearly dependent. \n\nTo see this, consider the set \n\n$$\n\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_1 + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} + 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + x_{n-1} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} + x_n \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution $x_1=x_2=\\ldots = x_{n-1}=0$ and $x_n=1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=\\ldots = x_n=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121125 #edb/ref 1065 6814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121125 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7270de59-1d83-5820-aa64-a5f4ca262e3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121125 #edb/ref 1067 "General Sets of Vectors Containing the Zero Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238138 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186238138 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238138 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "07a90ddc-0259-585f-9ae4-5617d9867886" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238174 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186238174 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238174 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0db0933f-2b18-50f8-b5dd-6e96fe942217" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238938 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186238938 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238938 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "650d4b10-6c78-5b66-b132-1ec8fff7e67b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239159 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186239159 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239159 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7ef45d2d-b0a6-5ab6-8951-91ce778b4f29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239822 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186239822 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239822 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c93b0e47-a83c-526d-8bd4-d6e268f20920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240753 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240753 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240753 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186239822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240753 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f14146b4-26f2-5e89-a173-b8e10576985c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241026 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241026 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105294" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241026 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241026 #edb/ref 1071 "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186241026 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9add2d2a-fce4-5ca8-9b10-b0aa51d393b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241026 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}=(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=-2{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}-2{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186241036 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186448902 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186241036 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186448903 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186241036 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186448904 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186241036 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105315" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241036 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241036 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\n- **II.** The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- **III.** The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186241036 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e39d7a95-6796-5061-a2aa-706f3f881251" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241036 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}-12{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -5 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+3(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}+2(0)+5(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, while statement I is false." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186275376 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450820 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275376 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450821 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275376 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450822 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275376 #edb/ref 1069 "q-157162" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275376 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275376 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n- **III.** The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186275376 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8e786624-3795-5bd2-9044-40e13fec09b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275376 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =-2\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186275386 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450838 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275386 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450839 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275386 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450840 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275386 #edb/ref 1069 "q-157252" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275386 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275386 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-6{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=0.$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- **III.** The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186275386 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2d5864d3-92dc-56c9-ae91-3d7c6bdd2052" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275386 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-{a}_{3} & =-6\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12-8-4 \\\\ -6+4+2 \\\\ -12+7+5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=6,\\,{x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{3}=1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186275388 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450844 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275388 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450845 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275388 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450846 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275388 #edb/ref 1069 "q-157256" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275388 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275388 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n- **III.** The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186275388 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e89e99e-07af-5529-a0c3-3af7fe4158cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275388 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =4\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186363384 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452518 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186363384 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452519 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186363384 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452520 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186363384 #edb/ref 1069 "q-157239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363384 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363384 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $5{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3}=0.$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- **III.** The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186363384 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cd19f076-6276-574d-a171-58c6f862f950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363384 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =5\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5-2-3 \\\\ -10+1+9 \\\\ -5-1+6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=5,\\,{x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{3}=3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186396694 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396694 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396694 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396694 #edb/ref 1071 "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186396694 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a792333-1cd7-596e-9f36-ea2d3aa5343e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396694 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}=4\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=4{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}-4{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 4{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{a}_{1}+(-1){a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=4$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186396695 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396695 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396695 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396695 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396695 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 1